\tag{fdip}
\title{Matter transported by finite-group links}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{A coefficient at one corner of a square can be carried to the
opposite corner along either pair of edges. The two values can
differ even when every individual transport is invertible. How
does that difference enter a first-order operator, its kernel and
its finite inverse, and how does its determinant weight a sum
over link assignments?}

